course: geography
lesson: 61
title: The map of the Just So Stories: from the North Atlantic to the Limpopo, the Amazon and Surrey
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Kipling's «Just So Stories» are made up, but the places in them are real, and you can find almost every one on a map: a spot in the open Atlantic, a river in southern Africa, a desert island near the Horn of Africa, the Amazon, the middle of Australia, the coast of Malaysia and a quiet valley in Surrey.
explain: Kipling knew the world well. He was born in Bombay (now Mumbai) in India in 1865, went to school in England, and later lived in America and spent many winters in South Africa, so his animals wander across the whole globe.
explain: The Whale is sent to «latitude Fifty North, longitude Forty West», which is a real address on the globe: 50 degrees north of the Equator and 40 degrees west of Greenwich, out in the cold North Atlantic between Ireland and Canada, far from any land.
explain: Reading the book with an atlas open turns a bedtime story into a journey: follow the Elephant's Child down to the Limpopo, or the Kangaroo across Australia, and check what is really there.
fact: Latitude says how far north or south of the Equator a place is, and longitude says how far east or west of Greenwich in London; 50° North, 40° West is in the open North Atlantic, between Ireland and Canada, and Cornwall in south-west England lies at about 50° North, 5° West.
fact: The Whale carries the sailor home to «the white-cliffs-of-Albion»: Albion is an old name for Britain, and the white cliffs of Dover are made of chalk.
fact: The Rhinoceros lives by the Red Sea, the long, narrow sea between Africa and Arabia; Socotra, named in the story's poem, is an island of Yemen, north of the Equator, near Cape Guardafui, the very tip of the Horn of Africa, and it is famous for its strange umbrella-shaped dragon's blood trees.
fact: The Leopard's «High Veld» is the Highveld, a high, flat grassland in South Africa mostly between about 1,200 and 2,000 metres above the sea; «veld» means open country in Afrikaans.
fact: The Limpopo is a real river, about 1,750 kilometres long, that forms the northern border of South Africa with Botswana and Zimbabwe and flows into the Indian Ocean in Mozambique; its banks really are lined with yellow-barked fever trees.
fact: Going north, the Elephant's Child goes through Graham's Town (now called Makhanda), near the south coast, and then Kimberley, further inland in South Africa, where diamond miners dug the huge «Big Hole», and through Khama's Country, which is now Botswana.
fact: Old Man Kangaroo is chased from the middle of Australia, mostly hot desert and dry grassland, past the Flinders and the Darling Downs; most Australians live near the coasts, where it rains more.
fact: The Armadillos' «turbid Amazon» is the Amazon in South America (turbid means muddy); it carries more water than any other river on Earth and flows through the biggest rainforest in the world.
fact: The Crab's story names the Perak river, Malacca and Singapore on the Malay Peninsula, by the Strait of Malacca, the short way for ships between the Indian Ocean and the seas of China, and one of the busiest shipping lanes in the world.
fact: The First Letter and the Alphabet stories happen in Surrey, south of London, near Guildford and the River Wey, a small river that flows into the Thames.
fact: The Eldest Magician makes the Himalayas, the Sahara and the Everglades in a game, but really the Himalayas were pushed up as India crashed into Asia over about 50 million years, and India still pushes, so they still rise a little; the Sahara was green with lakes and grass from about 11,000 until about 5,000 years ago, and rock paintings there show swimmers and cattle.
fact: The Everglades in Florida are a very wide, slow river of grass over flat land; Suleiman-bin-Daoud's story takes place in an imagined palace, and the real Queen of Sheba's land, Saba, was probably in Yemen.
exercise: What does latitude tell you? | How far north or south of the Equator a place is | How far east or west of London a place is | How high above the level of the sea a town or hill is | How hot a place usually is in summer
exercise: Where is latitude 50° North, longitude 40° West? | In the North Atlantic, between Ireland and Canada | In the middle of the hot Sahara Desert, in Africa | In the Pacific Ocean, near Japan | At the North Pole, on the ice
exercise: What is Albion? | An old name for Britain | A city in South Africa | A kind of whale | The old name for the Atlantic
exercise: What are the white cliffs of Dover made of? | Chalk | Ice | Granite | Salt
exercise: Which sea lies between Africa and Arabia? | The Red Sea | The Black Sea | The North Sea | The Dead Sea
exercise: Which island near the Horn of Africa has dragon's blood trees? | Socotra | Iceland | Madagascar | Sicily
exercise: What is the Highveld? | A high, flat grassland in South Africa | A thick, wet rainforest by the Amazon | A long sandy beach on the Red Sea | A snowy mountain range in India
exercise: Which countries does the Limpopo River separate South Africa from? | Botswana and Zimbabwe | Egypt and Sudan | Brazil and Peru | Spain and France, in Europe
exercise: Into which ocean does the Limpopo flow? | The Indian Ocean | The Atlantic Ocean | The Arctic Ocean | The Pacific Ocean
exercise: What is Khama's Country called today? | Botswana | Australia | Malaysia | Yemen
exercise: What is the «Big Hole» at Kimberley? | A huge pit dug by diamond miners | A lake made by a falling star | A cave where the elephants sleep | A crater left by an old volcano
exercise: Where do most Australians live? | Near the coasts, where it rains more | In the hot desert in the middle | On the Darling Downs only | On small islands far out at sea
exercise: Which river carries the most water on Earth? | The Amazon | The Limpopo | The Thames | The Nile
exercise: Which busy sea passage lies beside Malacca and Singapore? | The Strait of Malacca | The Strait of Gibraltar | The English Channel | The Bering Strait
exercise: In which English county do Taffy and Tegumai live? | Surrey | Yorkshire | Cornwall | Kent
exercise: What really pushed up the Himalayas? | India crashing slowly into Asia | A magician playing a game | A giant volcano blowing up | Wind piling up sand for ages
test: «latitude and longitude are the same thing really» What would you say? | No: latitude is north-south, longitude is east-west | Yes, they are two names for the same line on a map | No: latitude is for the sea and longitude is for land | No: latitude is height and longitude is width
test: ★★ The Whale swims due east from 50° North, 40° West, keeping to 50° North, until longitude is 5° West. Where is it now? | Near Cornwall, in south-west England | In the middle of the Sahara Desert | Off the coast of Australia | At the South Pole
test: «the Limpopo isn't real, it's a made-up name like Nqong» What is right? | It is a real river in southern Africa | It is real, but it is in South America | It is a made-up river Kipling drew on his map | It is a real lake in Scotland
test: A friend says the Sahara has always been a desert. What do rock paintings of swimmers and cattle show? | It was green with lakes about 5,000 to 11,000 years ago | It was always an icy desert, just like Antarctica is now | People painted things they saw only in their dreams | People swam in the sand in the old days
test: «the middle of Australia is jungle innit, that's where the roos are» What is true? | It is mostly hot desert and dry grassland | It is a wet, steamy rainforest like the Amazon | It is covered in deep snow most of the year | It is the sea, with islands
test: In which order would the Elephant's Child meet these places going north from the coast: Graham's Town, Kimberley, the Limpopo? | Graham's Town, Kimberley, then the Limpopo | The Limpopo, Kimberley, then Graham's Town | Kimberley, the Limpopo, then Graham's Town | The Limpopo, Graham's Town, then Kimberley
test: «the Himalayas are finished growing, they're old» What is true? | They are still rising a little, as India keeps pushing | They stopped growing millions of years ago | They are sinking slowly back into the sea a little every year | They grow only when there is an earthquake
test: A ship sails from India to China. Why does it go past Singapore? | The Strait of Malacca is the short way between oceans | Ships must stop in Singapore to ask the Crab | It is the only place where ships can buy fuel | The sea around India is frozen in winter
test: Kipling was born in Bombay. What is the city called today? | Mumbai | Delhi | Dhaka | Kolkata
test: ★★★ Socotra, Kimberley and the Limpopo are all in the Southern Hemisphere. Which one is wrong? | Socotra, which is north of the Equator | Kimberley, which is north of the Equator | The Limpopo, which is north of the Equator | None, all three are south of the Equator
test: «yo the Amazon's a website not a river» What would you say? | It's a real river in South America too, the biggest on Earth by water | It is only a website, the river was in a film | It is a small river in England named after the shop | It is a desert in Australia
exam: Identify the location designated by the coordinates fifty degrees north and forty degrees west. | A position in the open North Atlantic Ocean, remote from any coast | A position in the interior of the African continent | A position in the Indian Ocean adjacent to Socotra | A position upon the Antarctic ice sheet
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Limpopo River? | It forms part of the frontier of South Africa and discharges into the Indian Ocean | It rises in the Himalayas and discharges into the Bay of Bengal | It is a fictitious watercourse devised for the purposes of the tale | It traverses the Highveld and discharges into the Atlantic Ocean
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the formation of the Himalayas. | They were raised by the collision of the Indian landmass with Asia over tens of millions of years | They were deposited by the wind in the course of a single season | They were formed by the eruption of one great volcano | They were raised by the ancient Egyptians as a defence
exam: Ascertain the meaning of the word «turbid» as applied to the Amazon. | Muddy | Frozen | Narrow | Salty
